.TH SISTA-COORDINATES 3 "2026-10-09" "Sista"
.SH NAME
sista-coordinates \- represent a row and column in a Sista field
.SH LIBRARY
Sista C++ library (libSista, \fI-lSista\fP)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sista/coordinates.hpp>

.B "struct sista::Coordinates {"
.B "    unsigned short y;"
.B "    unsigned short x;"
.B "    Coordinates();"
.B "    Coordinates(unsigned short y, unsigned short x);"
.B "    static Coordinates fromPair(const std::pair<unsigned short,"
.B "                              unsigned short> &pair);"
.B "    std::pair<unsigned short, unsigned short> toPair() const;"
.B "};"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Coordinates
stores a zero-based row in
.B y
and a zero-based column in
.BR x .
It supports equality, row-major ordering, arithmetic, conversion to and
from
.BR std::pair ,
and hashing for standard unordered containers.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Arithmetic operators return coordinates and do not enforce field bounds.
.SH CAVEATS
The members are unsigned.
Subtraction can underflow, and arithmetic can produce a coordinate
invalid for a particular field.
.PP
The system of coordinates in Sista is
.B {y, x}
where y is increasing on the vertical axis as it gets to the bottom of the screen,
x is increasing on the horizontal axis as it gets to the right of the screen,
and both are zero based.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <sista/coordinates.hpp>

int main()
{
    sista::Coordinates position(2, 3);
    auto pair = position.toPair();
    return pair.first == 2 && pair.second == 3 ? 0 : 1;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sista-field (3),
.BR sista-pawn (3),
.BR sista (7)
